<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="S-adenosyl-L-methionine salvage II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5041" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; About 20% of the |FRAME: MET| pool is used as a building block of proteins. The rest is converted to |FRAME:S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE| (SAM), the major methyl donor in the cell. When SAM donates its methyl group, it is converted to |FRAME: ADENOSYL-HOMO-CYS|. This molecule can be recycled back to SAM via the &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-adenosyl-L-methionine cycle, also known as the activated methyl cycle (AMC). There are two main variations of this pathway, one found mostly in prokaryotes, while the other is found predominantly, but not exclusively, in eukaryotes (for example, it operates in |FRAME: TAX-1773| |CITS: [18815415]|. The main difference between the variants is the processing of |FRAME: ADENOSYL-HOMO-CYS| (SAH), the immediate product of the methylation reactions. In the first pathway (described in |FRAME: PWY-6151|) SAH is first hydrolyzed to |FRAME: CPD-564| by |FRAME: EC-3.2.2.9|, followed by conversion to |FRAME: HOMO-CYS| by |FRAME: EC-4.4.1.21|. In the second pathway, which is described in |FRAME: PWY-5041|, SAH is hydrolyzed to |FRAME: HOMO-CYS| in a single step, catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-3.13.2.1|. The cycle continues with the methylation of |FRAME: HOMO-CYS| to |FRAME: MET| using a methyl group from a methylated folate. In some organisms, including |FRAME: TAX-9606|, this reaction is catalyzed by a |FRAME: HOMOCYSMETB12-MONOMER| (EC 2.1.1.13). In other organisms, such as |FRAME: TAX-1423|, the reaction is catalyzed by a |FRAME: G18NG-10711-MONOMER| (EC 2.1.1.14). Yet some organisms, such as |FRAME: TAX-562| and |FRAME: TAX-1718|, have both enzymes, as described in the pathway |FRAME: HSERMETANA-PWY|. In |FRAME: TAX-562| the reaction catalyzed by the B12-dependent enzyme is more than 100-fold faster than the reaction catalyzed by the B12-independent isoenzyme |CITS: [15215334]|. Finally, the cycle is completed with the regeneration of SAM by |FRAME: CPLX-7754|. About |FRAME: CPLX-5161|: While the reaction catalyzed by this enzyme is reversible, the equilibrium favors the synthesis of |FRAME: ADENOSYL-HOMO-CYS|. Thus the removal of the hydrolysis product |FRAME: HOMO-CYS| is critical for the continuous operation of the cycle. This is achieved by the efficient conversion of |FRAME: HOMO-CYS| to |FRAME: MET| via |FRAME: AT5G17920-MONOMER|.</Description>
